@node debug95___fat95_state_dump "debug95 - fat95 state dump"
@{b}@{u}debug95 - fat95 state dump@{uu}@{ub}

@{fg shine}debug95@{fg text} writes the internal state of fat95 for one volume to a file.
Attach the file to a bug report.

Reading the file is expert work. @{fg shine}parse_debug95.py@{fg text} from the @{fg shine}tools@{fg text}
directory of the source repository
(https://github.com/pulchart/fat95/tree/master/tools) turns it into text
on a PC with Python 3.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Main}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Usage}

@endnode

@node Usage "Usage"
@{b}Usage@{ub}

@{fg highlight}
  debug95 <volume> <file>
  
  debug95 CF0: RAM:cf0.dump
@{fg text}

The only condition is that the handler can still answer a packet.
debug95 gives up after 10 seconds without an answer, and Ctrl-C stops it
earlier.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link debug95___fat95_state_dump}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Reading_the_dump}

@endnode

@node Reading_the_dump "Reading the dump"
@{b}Reading the dump@{ub}

Take the parser from the same release as the handler that wrote the
dump, because each release can lay out its variables differently. A dump
of another release makes the parser print a warning.

@{fg highlight}
  python3 tools/parse_debug95.py cf0.dump                 summary
  python3 tools/parse_debug95.py --all cf0.dump           every variable
  python3 tools/parse_debug95.py --diff a.dump b.dump     variables that differ
@{fg text}

@{fg shine}--diff@{fg text} compares two dumps.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Usage}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link The_summary}

@endnode

@node The_summary "The summary"
@{b}The summary@{ub}

@{fg highlight}
  fat95 4.3 handler state, cf0.dump (1232 bytes)
  
  Device     unit                        0
             block size                  512 bytes
             sector size                 512 bytes
             open flags                  0x00000000
             features                    TD64 commands available
                                         use CMD_UPDATE
  
  Partition  type                        FAT32
             DosType                     FAT\255
             number                      1
             first block                 2048
             blocks                      131072
             size                        64 MiB
             cluster size                512 bytes
             mounted by another handler  no
  
  Volume     name                        "FAT32"
             serial number               0x57C0733A
             disk type                   FAT\255
             state                       validated
             dirty flag in use           yes
             mounted clean               yes
             free                        62.8 MiB
             write locked                no
  
  Activity   open locks                  0
             inhibit count               0
             buffers                     50
  
  Names      [unicode] table             no
             [oem] table                 yes
  
  Errors     last DOS error              none
             last read                   none
             last block read             4098
             failed reads                0
             write fault                 no
@{fg text}

@{b}Device@{ub} shows how fat95 talks to the device. The features list the
command sets it found (TD64, ETD or SCSI) and the options it runs with.

@{b}Partition@{ub} shows which partition fat95 picked, where it starts and how
large it is. Number 0 means fat95 found no partition table and uses the
medium or the RDB partition as a whole.

@{b}Volume@{ub} shows the volume as AmigaDOS sees it. Mounted clean is no for a
volume that was dirty at mount, and such a volume stays dirty.

@{b}Activity@{ub} counts the locks and files still open, which keep a volume from
being unmounted.

@{b}Names@{ub} shows whether the language file supplied the @{fg shine}[unicode]@{fg text} and @{fg shine}[oem]@{fg text}
tables that convert file names.

@{b}Errors@{ub} keeps the last DOS error, the last device error with the block it
read, and how many reads failed. A write fault blocks every further
write until the volume is mounted again.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Reading_the_dump}  @{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode
